                        <!-- Interview questions use the new white text style defined in CSS -->
                        <h4>1. What AI innovation or project are you currently most excited about?</h4>
                        <p>I’m most excited about the <b>RFP Gem</b>, which I think most of the legal folks have heard of. After an intensive 6-month journey, we are on the verge of formally piloting and eventually launching it with our sales and proposals teams, and I couldn’t be more excited. I’ve been its champion from day one. The initial spark came from a quarterly sync with Alice, and from there, I’ve had the privilege of leading the charge. Building it from the ground up. It's been a marathon, but I want to be clear that this has been a massive team effort with incredible collaboration and support from all our stakeholders.</p>
                        
                        <h4>2. Can you walk us through the problem this AI solution addresses and how it provides value to our team or users?</h4>
                        <p>Our current go/no-go process is a manual, multi-stakeholder review that can consume dozens of man-hours per RFP. It's a non-standardized process that results in subjective, high-variance risk assessment and, critically, yields no analyzable data. Additionally, it is only utilized by one subset of the sales organization, if it’s utilized at all.</p>
                        <p>The <b>RFP Gem</b> is designed to fix this systemically. It leverages a fine-tuned model to perform analysis on complex terms. It parses entire RFP documents and intake forms, extracting and classifying key terms against a predefined matrix of our core legal, security, and business model requirements. It then generates a quantitative risk score and a data-backed 'Go/No-Go' recommendation.</p>
                        <p>For our Teams: It compresses the initial review cycle from days to minutes. Sales, Legal, and Proposals get an instant, objective, and standardized risk score. This creates a unified output and recommendation, aligning all stakeholders around a single, auditable report from the very start. This means that we will not only have a better grasp on what RFPs we should proceed with, but the entire sales organization can adopt the process.</p>
                        <p>For Procore: This enables scalable growth by decoupling our review bandwidth from RFP volume. It shifts our valuable SME hours from low-value textual extraction to high-value strategic negotiation. Most importantly, it allows for future implementation of data-driven governance. This will provide a structured dataset from unstructured documents for the first time. We can aggregate this data to answer strategic questions like, "What percentage of enterprise RFPs are flagged for uncapped liability?" This transforms risk management from a reactive, deal-by-deal function into a proactive, data-driven corporate strategy.</p>
                        
                        <h4>3. What was the biggest technical challenge you faced during this project or task?</h4>
                        <p>The real hurdle was translating the deep, human expertise from our internal teams into a language the AI could actually execute reliably. It’s not just about keywords; it’s about concepts. How do you teach an AI to understand the intent behind dozens of different clauses? I quickly learned the AI would sometimes drop instructions or miss a subtle nuance. The solution wasn't a single "fix." It was countless hours of testing, refining, and collaborating closely with the AI itself and our internal experts to fine-tune the prompts until it was rock-solid.</p>
                        
                        <h4>4. In one sentence, how would you describe the impact of this innovation on our team's workflow or future direction?</h4>
                        <p>This gem moves our review process from a manual, subjective bottleneck to a high-speed, data-driven function, instantly aligning Legal, Sales, and Proposals with a single source of truth thus reducing inter-team friction and instead promoting collaboration.</p>
                        
                        <h4>5. How do you stay current with the rapidly evolving field of AI?</h4>
                        <p>You can't just be a spectator in this field. Besides reading articles and talking with colleagues, I believe you have to be hands-on. I'm constantly experimenting. I’m testing new tools, pushing prompts to see where they break, and even revisiting old prompts to see how the underlying models have improved. The field is moving too fast for just theory; you have to "live it" through daily trial and error.</p>
                        
                        <h4>6. What is your personal philosophy on the role of AI in our industry?</h4>
                        <p>I’ll be the first to admit I was an AI skeptic. I had the same "will it replace me?" anxiety many people feel. My entire mindset shifted during Paul’s prompting seminar at the legal team offsite, where I had an "a-ha!" moment: AI isn't a replacement; it's an <b>amplifier</b>.</p>
                        <p>My philosophy now is that AI is a powerful tool in our toolkit. It's not a cure-all, and it absolutely requires human oversight. It’s here to handle the repetitive work, which frees us up to focus on the critical work that requires deep human expertise and strategic nuance.</p>
                        <p>What excites me most is watching AI become more concept-aware. It’s already moving beyond simple keyword matching to understand meaning, like knowing "indemnify without limitation" is the concept of "<b>uncapped liability</b>." As that capability grows, it will build incredible trust between users and AI, and unlock entirely new ways for us to work smarter.</p>
                        
                        <h4>7. Is there a tool, library, or technique you've recently adopted that has fundamentally changed the way you approach your work?</h4>
                        <p>I’ve most recently adopted the <b>Improve Prompt</b> feature in Harvey. This feature utilizes AI before the response generation. Good input is one of, if not the most, important components of AI use. The improve prompt feature works to make your prompt clearer, more thorough, and more AI friendly. The best part is, it can be applied to any AI platform. I will go into Harvey, pop in my prompt, and use the improve prompt feature. I think rework the prompt and bring it over to Gemini if I think that’s the more appropriate platform for the project/task. This has allowed for exponentially better input on my end, meaning better base output and less reworking to try and get the AI to understand what I am asking it.</p>
                        
